#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082942 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205ecbfe-49fd-559f-a4e7-a36900f1d9c7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "17f80459-f98c-5f34-acd4-6407c047615e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14835" :question/problem "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=2x^2-12x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=2$ and $b=-12$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-12)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-12)}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e00837a5-acbf-563d-864a-2902fc8e02a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex of a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "f4cf6319-af75-53c4-a5a8-87478b7c3fd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14836" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-x^2+4x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-1$ and $b=4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{4}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=2.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}+4x+1 \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+8+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(2,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1ed6e03-7d4f-5606-ab01-3bfdd855348e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Coefficient Given the Axis of Symmetry"}] :question/id #uuid "dad0da4b-af03-507c-9549-6f5963783da6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14837" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=0.25x^2+kx+2$ is $x=-6.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $-6.$\n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=0.25$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(0.25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{0.5} \\\\  & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-6,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e00837a5-acbf-563d-864a-2902fc8e02a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex of a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1546d0b3-484a-554f-a971-19b0784fdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539521} {:answer/id #uuid "388620da-b528-5628-804b-351a30e184bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-118)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539583} {:answer/id #uuid "7727e947-2e9c-5fb6-9177-9b23822a088a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-118)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539678} {:answer/id #uuid "9cddc5f1-bf50-5346-a250-f95ea0170398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539738} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0f143d-af04-5bca-8cf5-7e1465de78d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd0f143d-af04-5bca-8cf5-7e1465de78d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539857} :answer-field/id #uuid "9031e136-f3f0-5899-8f2b-3f34c3da7fea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c18c5b5e-e1be-5b37-80b1-242d38a7007c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106548" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-4{x}^{2}-24x-10.$" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-4$ and $b=-24$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-24)}{2(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-24)}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4{x}^{2}-24x-10 \\\\  & =-4(-3)^{2}-24(-3)-10 \\\\  & =-36+72-10 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-3,26).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205ecbfe-49fd-559f-a4e7-a36900f1d9c7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171eba40-f441-599d-bfb0-408b5950a3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "171eba40-f441-599d-bfb0-408b5950a3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539526} :answer-field/id #uuid "70a59569-7026-55cb-8f99-3bedbd6a3b2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b00cf238-5726-569e-8376-bed95c1380a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-222079" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}+10x-3$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=-2$ and $b=10$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{10}{2(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1ed6e03-7d4f-5606-ab01-3bfdd855348e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Coefficient Given the Axis of Symmetry"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6f131a8-f416-56ab-b966-abc820357c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6f131a8-f416-56ab-b966-abc820357c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539800} :answer-field/id #uuid "a61a87ea-3783-51c0-97b6-886a2e7f21d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5ac6aea-bcfe-5a0e-83c5-58a351f6e0ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-222095" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ea5d11ca8b92193517fa7082a466e2ece1f5c505e6881c70ce8ebebb5b93913.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=5{x}^{2}+kx-8,$ shown above.\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2,$ we will set our result equal to $-2.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=5$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{10} & =-2 \\\\ -k & =-20 \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
